@page "/group-rides"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Rides
@inject IApiClient Api
@inject ConfirmService Confirm
@* §ClockRules: never DateTime.Now — the wait below is measured against the injected clock. *@
@inject TimeProvider Clock
@* Leaving takes the rail's globe with it when it was pointing at this ride (§18.6), and the
   device's own copy of it (§4.4) — a ride nobody is on must not still open from a cache. *@
@inject CurrentRideState CurrentRide
@inject RideSnapshotCache Cache

<PageTitle>Group adventures — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

@*
	The signed-in entry point to group rides (§5.2).

	Sections split server-side (§5.2), so a member of one ride and the organiser of
	another gets two lists rather than one list plus a filter. Both show the join
	code — a rider who has joined needs it to tell somebody else how to follow along, and
	the badge is the only place they can read it back off.

	The third list is the odd one out and is meant to be: an adventure the rider has asked to
	join and not been let into is not theirs yet. Its rows do not open, they carry no join code,
	and the section is absent entirely when nobody is waiting on anything.
*@

<div class="page page-medium">
	<PageNav Title="Group adventures" BackHref="/" BackLabel="Back to home" />

	<div class="cards">
		<a class="card" href="/group-rides/create">
			<h2>Create an adventure</h2>
			<p>Start an adventure of your own. You will be the organiser.</p>
		</a>
		<a class="card" href="/group-rides/join">
			<h2>Join an adventure</h2>
			<p>Enter a code the organiser gave you.</p>
		</a>
	</div>

	@*
		Above the lists rather than instead of them. A failed *load* has nothing to show under it
		anyway, but a refused delete is a message about one row of a list the reader is still
		looking at — replacing the list with the sentence would take away the thing it is about.
	*@
	@if (_error is not null)
	{
		<p class="error">@_error</p>
	}

	@if (_rides is null)
	{
		@if (_error is null)
		{
			<p class="empty">Loading…</p>
		}
	}
	else
	{
		@*
			Waiting on somebody else's decision (§5.2).

			First, because it is the only list on this page with an open question in it. The other
			two are things the rider can already do; this one is the one they came back to check.

			Rendered only when it has rows, unlike the two below it. Those say "you have not created
			any adventures yet" because being empty is a state worth naming to somebody who has just
			arrived — it is what the two cards at the top are an answer to. This one has no such
			job: almost nobody ever has a pending request, and a permanent "you are not waiting on
			anything" is a line every rider reads forever so that a few can read it once.
		*@
		@if (_rides.Waiting.Count > 0)
		{
			<section class="ride-section waiting">
				<h2>Waiting for approval</h2>
				<ul class="ride-list">
					@foreach (WaitingRide waiting in _rides.Waiting)
					{
						@*
							A li and not an anchor. Every other row on this page opens a ride; this one
							cannot, because the caller is not on it — the detail endpoint answers a
							non-member the same 404 a stranger gets (§5.2), so a link here would be a
							control that leads to a dead end. Nothing about the row invites a tap.
						*@
						<li class="ride pending">
							<div class="ride-link">
								<h2>@waiting.Name</h2>
								<span class="meta">
									@waiting.StartUtc.ToLocalTime().ToString("ddd d MMM yyyy, h:mm tt")
									· @waiting.State
								</span>
								@*
									How long they have been waiting, which is the thing this list is
									really for. "Asked 3 days ago" is what tells somebody the organiser
									is never going to answer; a date on its own leaves them counting.
								*@
								<span class="meta asked">Asked @Ago(waiting.RequestedUtc)</span>
							</div>

							@* No join code here, and none available to put here — see WaitingRide. *@
							<span class="pending-badge" title="The organiser has not answered yet">
								<i class="fa fa-fw fa-hourglass-half" aria-hidden="true"></i>
								Pending
							</span>

							@*
								The way out of a wait. Until this existed the only way to stop waiting was to
								be answered, which for a ride whose organiser has gone quiet is never.

								Not `danger`, unlike the organiser's trash beside it: nothing is destroyed
								here but a request nobody answered, and the rider can ask again with the same
								code the moment they change their mind back. The trash is red because it takes
								an adventure away from everybody; this one is the smallest undo in the app.
							*@
							<button type="button" class="withdraw" disabled="@(_leaving == waiting.RideId)"
									title="Withdraw this request"
									aria-label="@($"Withdraw your request to join {waiting.Name}")"
									@onclick="() => WithdrawAsync(waiting)">
								<i class="fa fa-fw fa-xmark" aria-hidden="true"></i>
							</button>
						</li>
					}
				</ul>
			</section>
		}

		<section class="ride-section">
			<h2>Organised by you</h2>
			@if (_rides.Organised.Count == 0)
			{
				<p class="empty">You have not created any adventures yet.</p>
			}
			else
			{
				<ul class="ride-list">
					@foreach (RideSummary ride in _rides.Organised)
					{
						<li class="ride">
							<a class="ride-link" href="/group-rides/live/@ride.Id">
								<h2>@ride.Name</h2>
								<span class="meta">
									@ride.StartUtc.ToLocalTime().ToString("ddd d MMM yyyy, h:mm tt")
									· @ride.MemberCount member@(ride.MemberCount == 1 ? "" : "s")
									· @ride.State
								</span>
							</a>
							@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(ride.JoinCode))
							{
								<span class="join-code" title="Join code — share with people you invite">
									@ride.JoinCode
								</span>
							}
							@*
								Organised only. Deleting is the organiser's verb; a member who wants
								out has Leave, on the ride's own screen, and it takes their position
								with them rather than everybody else's day.
							*@
							<button type="button" class="danger" disabled="@(_deleting == ride.Id)"
									title="Delete this adventure" aria-label="@($"Delete {ride.Name}")"
									@onclick="() => DeleteAsync(ride)">
								<i class="fa fa-fw fa-trash" aria-hidden="true"></i>
							</button>
						</li>
					}
				</ul>
			}
		</section>

		<section class="ride-section">
			<h2>Joined</h2>
			@if (_rides.Joined.Count == 0)
			{
				<p class="empty">You have not joined any adventures yet.</p>
			}
			else
			{
				<ul class="ride-list">
					@foreach (RideSummary ride in _rides.Joined)
					{
						<li class="ride">
							<a class="ride-link" href="/group-rides/live/@ride.Id">
								<h2>@ride.Name</h2>
								<span class="meta">
									@ride.StartUtc.ToLocalTime().ToString("ddd d MMM yyyy, h:mm tt")
									· @ride.MemberCount member@(ride.MemberCount == 1 ? "" : "s")
									· @ride.State
								</span>
							</a>
							@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(ride.JoinCode))
							{
								<span class="join-code" title="Join code — share with people you invite">
									@ride.JoinCode
								</span>
							}

							@*
								Leaving, from the list rather than only from inside the ride (§5.6).

								It was reachable before this — the ride's own info page has "Leave adventure…"
								— but only by opening the ride first, which is the wrong shape for the case it
								is most wanted in: a rider tidying up several adventures they are done with.
								The confirm is the same one, and it is the confirm rather than the placement
								that makes this safe.

								Only on this list. The organised rows have the trash instead, and the server
								refuses to let an organiser leave their own ride outright (409, "a ride nobody
								organises has nobody to decide who is in it") — so a Leave there would be a
								control that cannot work.
							*@
							<button type="button" class="leave" disabled="@(_leaving == ride.Id)"
									title="Leave this adventure"
									aria-label="@($"Leave {ride.Name}")"
									@onclick="() => LeaveAsync(ride)">
								<i class="fa fa-fw fa-right-from-bracket" aria-hidden="true"></i>
							</button>
						</li>
					}
				</ul>
			}
		</section>
	}
</div>

@code {
	private MyRides? _rides;
	private string? _error;

	// Which row's trash was pressed, rather than one page-wide flag: the list is several rides
	// and greying all of them out because one is going says the wrong thing about the others.
	private Guid? _deleting;

	// The same idea for the two ways off a ride. One field for both, because a row is either a
	// membership or a request and never both — and it is keyed by ride id in both cases, so a
	// rider who is somehow on one list and waiting on the other still only ever locks one row.
	private Guid? _leaving;

	protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => await LoadAsync();

	/// <summary>
	/// How long ago a request was made, in the words a rider would use.
	/// <para>
	/// Rounded down and coarse on purpose: the reader is deciding whether the organiser has
	/// forgotten about them, and "2 days ago" answers that where a timestamp makes them do the
	/// arithmetic. The same ladder <c>RideRequests</c> shows the organiser, so the two sides of
	/// one wait are described the same way.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string Ago(DateTimeOffset when)
	{
		TimeSpan waited = Clock.GetUtcNow() - when;

		if (waited < TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1)) return "just now";
		if (waited < TimeSpan.FromHours(1)) return $"{(int)waited.TotalMinutes} min ago";
		if (waited < TimeSpan.FromDays(1)) return $"{(int)waited.TotalHours} h ago";

		return $"{(int)waited.TotalDays} d ago";
	}

	private async Task LoadAsync()
	{
		try
		{
			_rides = await Api.ListMyRidesAsync();
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_error = exception.Message;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Leaves an adventure the caller was admitted to (§5.6).
	/// <para>
	/// The same three consequences the ride's own Leave has, because it is the same act: the
	/// membership goes, the device's copy goes with it — a ride nobody is on must not still open
	/// from a cache (§4.4) — and the rail's globe stops pointing at it if that is where it pointed
	/// (§18.6). Doing two of the three would leave the app remembering a ride the server has
	/// forgotten.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The receiver is not touched here, unlike in <c>RideSession.LeaveRideAsync</c>. It is stopped
	/// per ride by the session that started it, and this page has none — a rider on this screen is
	/// not on a map, and the server has already stopped copying their position into a ride they
	/// left (§5.6). The next ride they open brings its own receiver up.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task LeaveAsync(RideSummary ride)
	{
		if (_leaving is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		bool confirmed = await Confirm.AskAsync(
			$"Leave {ride.Name}?",
			"Your location stops going to the group, the position the adventure holds for you is "
			+ "deleted, and it drops off this list. The adventure itself carries on without you. "
			+ "You would need the join code — or a new request — to get back on.",
			confirmText: "Leave");

		if (!confirmed)
		{
			return;
		}

		_leaving = ride.Id;
		_error = null;

		try
		{
			await Api.LeaveRideAsync(ride.Id);

			// Both before the refetch, so the list and the rail cannot disagree even for a render.
			await Cache.ForgetAsync(ride.Id);
			await CurrentRide.ForgetAsync(ride.Id);

			await LoadAsync();
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			// The one that lands here is the organiser's 409, which this list should never be able
			// to produce — the button is only on rides they joined. Shown rather than swallowed, on
			// the principle that a refusal the app did not expect is exactly the one worth reading.
			_error = $"Could not leave {ride.Name}: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_leaving = null;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Takes back a request nobody has answered (§5.2).
	/// <para>
	/// No cache and no globe to clear, unlike <see cref="LeaveAsync"/>: neither ever pointed at
	/// this adventure, because the rider was never on it. Withdrawing frees their pending slot and
	/// leaves them free to ask again with the same code.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Confirmed, though it destroys nothing. What it costs is the queue position and the
	/// organiser's attention — asking again puts them at the back of a list somebody has already
	/// read — and the button sits a thumb's width from nothing else on the row.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task WithdrawAsync(WaitingRide waiting)
	{
		if (_leaving is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		bool confirmed = await Confirm.AskAsync(
			$"Withdraw your request to join {waiting.Name}?",
			"The organiser stops seeing it. You can ask again with the same join code whenever "
			+ "you like.",
			confirmText: "Withdraw");

		if (!confirmed)
		{
			return;
		}

		_leaving = waiting.RideId;
		_error = null;

		try
		{
			await Api.WithdrawJoinRequestAsync(waiting.RideId, waiting.RequestId);
			await LoadAsync();
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_error = $"Could not withdraw your request to join {waiting.Name}: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_leaving = null;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Deletes an adventure the caller organises (§5.2), through the app's own dialog rather than
	/// <c>window.confirm</c> — it is irreversible, and the trash sits one thumb-width from the row
	/// that opens the ride.
	/// <para>
	/// The list is refetched rather than the row spliced out locally: the server is the one that
	/// knows what is left, and a refusal has to leave the row exactly where it was.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task DeleteAsync(RideSummary ride)
	{
		if (_deleting is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		bool confirmed = await Confirm.AskAsync(
			$"Delete {ride.Name}?",
			"The adventure, its planned routes, its markers and everything anybody posted to it "
			+ "are deleted from the server, for every member and not only for you. This cannot be "
			+ "undone. Ending the adventure instead stops the location sharing and keeps the day.",
			confirmText: "Delete",
			danger: true);

		if (!confirmed)
		{
			return;
		}

		_deleting = ride.Id;
		_error = null;

		try
		{
			await Api.DeleteRideAsync(ride.Id);
			await LoadAsync();
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			// The one that lands here in practice is the refusal on a ride in progress, and the
			// server says so in words worth showing.
			_error = $"Could not delete {ride.Name}: {exception.Message}";
		}
		finally
		{
			_deleting = null;
		}
	}
}

<style>
	.ride-section { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
	.ride-section h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
	.ride-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
	.ride {
		display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 1rem;
		padding: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; background: #fff2; border-radius: 6px;
	}
	.ride-link { flex: 1; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; }
	.ride-link:hover strong { text-decoration: underline; }
	.ride .meta { font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; }
	/* An icon button, so it states its own size rather than inheriting the page's text-button
	   padding. Square and thumb-sized, because it sits beside the link that opens the ride. */
	.ride .danger {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		min-width: 44px;
		min-height: 44px;
		padding: 0 0.6rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

	/*
		A row that is not a way in. Dashed rather than filled, and the heading quieter than the two
		lists below: an adventure nobody has admitted this rider to should not sit on the page
		looking exactly like the ones they can open.
	*/
	.ride.pending {
		background: transparent;
		border: 1px dashed var(--dlr-border);
	}

	/* The div in a pending row takes the flex share the anchor takes in the others, without
	   taking the pointer or the underline that go with being a link. */
	.ride.pending .ride-link { cursor: default; }

	.ride .asked { opacity: 0.6; }

	.ride .pending-badge {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.35rem;
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
		border-radius: 999px;
		background: #fef3c730;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	/*
		The two ways off a ride, sized like the trash beside them so the three read as one column
		down the right of the page rather than three different affordances.

		Neither is red. The trash takes an adventure away from everybody and says so in colour;
		these two take one rider off one ride, and both are undoable with a join code.
	*/
	.ride .leave, .ride .withdraw {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		min-width: 44px;
		min-height: 44px;
		padding: 0 0.6rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
		background: transparent;
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		border-radius: 6px;
		color: inherit;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

		.ride .leave:disabled, .ride .withdraw:disabled {
			opacity: 0.6;
			cursor: default;
		}

	.ride .join-code {
		font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
		font-size: 1rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: 0.05em;
		padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem; background: #dbeafe; color: #1e40af; border-radius: 4px;
	}
</style>
